jank for Debian
===============

This package is a snapshot of upstream's main branch (jank is alpha software),
built against Debian's clang-22.

Locale
------

jank decodes source files according to the locale. Run it in a UTF-8 locale
(e.g. LANG=C.UTF-8) if your sources contain non-ASCII characters; in the C
locale a string such as "café" fails with "Unterminated string".

clang-22
--------

jank uses /usr/bin/clang++-22 and clang-22's resource directory at runtime for
its JIT and for ahead-of-time compilation. The package depends on the same
upstream clang-22 release it was built with and is rebuilt when trixie moves to
a new clang-22 point release.

On first use jank builds a precompiled header into ~/.cache/jank/.

Ahead-of-time compilation
-------------------------

`jank compile` uses the static runtime by default, which needs nothing beyond
this package's dependencies. `--runtime dynamic` links the program against
libLLVM and libclang-cpp and needs llvm-22-dev and libclang-cpp22-dev (both
recommended packages; they provide the unversioned libLLVM.so and libclang-cpp.so).

gdb
---

Upstream's gdb helper for jank values is installed as
/usr/share/jank/gdb/jank.py; load it in gdb with
`source /usr/share/jank/gdb/jank.py`.

Known issues
------------

- `jank print-cflags` and the include search of the JIT contain directories of
  the build environment (/work/src/...), which do not exist on installed
  systems. They come before the system include directories, so headers placed
  there would take precedence in every JIT compilation and persist in the
  precompiled header in ~/.cache/jank. This is harmless as long as /work is not
  created by an untrusted party (on a normal system only root can create it).
  It comes from upstream's build and is to be reported upstream.
- Debian-specific patches: debian/patches/0001 lets jank build with Clang 22;
  debian/patches/0002 fixes a crash when jank is started by name from a
  directory containing an entry named "jank".
